-- title:
--   Extensionality for K-algebra maps out of the level-ℓ modular function field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $\ell$ be a natural number with $\ell \neq 0$, and consider the intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldC K ℓ` of the Laurent series field `LaurentSeries K`, namely the $K$-subextension generated by the two elements $j_q = q^{-1}\cdot \mathrm{jNum}$, where $\mathrm{jNum} = E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-1}\text{-unit}$ is the integral power series `jNum` with coefficients pushed into $K$ (so $j_q$ is the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant reduced into $K$), and its image $j_{q,\ell}$ under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K ℓ`, which multiplies all Hahn-series exponents by $\ell$ (the substitution $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$). Let $A$ be a semiring equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and let $f, g : \mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC } K\ \ell \to A$ be two $K$-algebra homomorphisms. Assume that $f$ and $g$ take the same value on the element of the intermediate field given by $j_q$ together with its membership witness, and likewise the same value on the element given by $j_{q,\ell}$. The conclusion is that $f = g$.
--
--   This is the extensionality principle attached to the classical presentation of the function field of $X_0(\ell)$ as generated by $j$ and $j_\ell = j(q^{\ell})$, here in the form of a $K$-generic statement about the subfield of $K((q))$ generated by the two $q$-expansions. It is used throughout the treatment of the modular curve to identify maps out of this field — for instance when comparing an arithmetic Frobenius action, when computing $q$-expansion coefficients of differentials, and in the counting of roots at places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_modularFunctionFieldC_algHom_ext.lean

